{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/RabbitMQ Event Bus.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/RabbitMQ Event Bus.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"rabbit mq event bus rabbit mq event bus","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/RabbitMQ Event Bus.md","fileName":"RabbitMQ Event Bus.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"RabbitMQ Event Bus","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-27 11:11\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[.NET Microservice Communication and Workers]]","tags":["baby"],"links":[".NET Microservice Communication and Workers","hands-onrestfulwebserviceswithaspnetcore3.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/RabbitMQ Event Bus.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/RabbitMQ Event Bus.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"rabbit mq event bus rabbit mq event bus","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/RabbitMQ Event Bus.md","fileName":"RabbitMQ Event Bus.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"RabbitMQ Event Bus","heading":"RabbitMQ Event Bus","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"A RabbitMQ event bus carries integration events from producing services to independently running consumers through exchanges and queues. The producer publishes a message without needing a direct HTTP connection to every service that reacts to it.\n\nThis communication model improves temporal and deployment decoupling, but it introduces delivery concerns. Consumers need explicit acknowledgement, retry, idempotency, and failure-handling rules because a message can arrive more than once or outlive the process that first receives it.","tags":["baby"],"links":[".NET Microservice Communication and Workers","hands-onrestfulwebserviceswithaspnetcore3.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/RabbitMQ Event Bus.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/RabbitMQ Event Bus.md